    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Talking about your phone",
     "texto": [
      "**Descargar** and **subir** are opposites: you download an **aplicación** and you upload a photo to **redes sociales**.\n\n- Your **cargador** charges the **batería**. If it fails, **te quedas sin batería**.\n- Without **cobertura** there is no internet. To join a network you need the **contraseña**.\n- In Spain people say **el móvil**; in Latin America, **el celular**.\n- If you cannot put your phone down, **estás enganchado**.\n\nTo read an email address aloud: **@** is **arroba**, the dot is **punto**, **-** is **guion** and **_** is **guion bajo**. So **ana.ruiz@correo.es** is read **ana punto ruiz arroba correo punto es**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The present perfect: what you have done today",
     "texto": [
      "To tell what you have just done, use **haber** + participle: **he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han** + **-ado / -ido**.\n\n- descargar → **he descargado**\n- subir → **has subido**\n- quedarse → **me he quedado**\n\nSome participles are irregular: **hecho, puesto, escrito, visto, roto**.\n\nThe participle does not change after **haber**. Words that often go with it: **ya**, **todavía no**, **hoy**, **esta semana**."
     ],
